WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.470
Even the might of the Hulk is no match for
the power of all six Infinity Stones.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.200
During a recent Twitter Q&amp;A with Wired, Avengers:
Endgame directors Joe and Anthony Russo revealed

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.450
the reason the Green Goliath failed to heal
after using the stones to resurrect the dusted

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.380
heroes in the film's final act.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.080
Simply put: the combined power of the stones
would have been too much for virtually any

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.340
being in existence to handle, and Bruce Banner
is probably lucky to still be breathing.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.960
You'll remember that in Endgame, Banner volunteered
to use the stones — housed inside a brand

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.579
spanking new, Tony Stark-built Infinity Gauntlet
2.0 — to reverse the destruction wrought

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.329
by Thanos' infamous snap at the conclusion
of Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.060
Banner was convinced of his destiny by the
stones' radiation signature, which was "mostly

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.760
gamma" — the same type of radiation that
endowed him with the power of the Hulk.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:47.740
"It's like, uh…

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.020
I was made for this."

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.560
But his snap, while successful, took a terrible
toll.

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:59.640
The arm on which he wore the gauntlet was
seen to be largely useless after his heroic

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:04.280
action, and fans looked to the Russos to explain
why such a powerful being would suffer such

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.680
shocking consequences from using the stones.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.030
Twitter user Victor Stone Meanmuggin tweeted:

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.180
"Hold on.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.980
The Hulk has a whole healing factor… why
didn't his arm heal?"

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:18.120
Anthony Russo replied that, healing factor
or no, using the combined power of the stones

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.890
is simply too much for any being to walk away
from — even Thanos, who famously slapped

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.040
the Hulk around like a rag doll in the opening
scene of Infinity War.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:27.260
Joe Russo explained:

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.030
"The Hulk has never come up against every
Infinity Stone in a gauntlet, and the pure

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:31.960
power of the stones.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.560
Thanos is nearly invincible, and he did not
heal, either.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.280
So, clearly, when you wield the full power
of the Infinity Stones, it does irreparable

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:39.540
damage to your being."

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.480
Then Anthony Russo added, quote, "If it doesn't
even kill you."

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:43.140
Oof.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.220
Too soon, man.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.680
Rest in peace, Mr. Stark…

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:50.150
Anywho, the ace directing duo also offered
some insight as to what led Banner to merge

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.160
the two halves of himself, which was a crucial
development — considering that it's likely

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.810
that nobody would have been able to talk the
Hulk into wielding the Stark Gauntlet, had

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.820
he not been possessed of Banner's personality
and intellect.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.900
"Eighteen months in the gamma lab.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.660
I put the brains and the brawn together.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.440
And now look at me.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.380
Best of both worlds!"

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.740
Anthony Russo explained:

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.160
"We wanted to track how every single one of
the Avengers was moving forward from the experience

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.100
of Infinity War.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.231
And Banner had this very unique experience,
where… in the opening of Infinity War, he

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.360
loses to Thanos as the Hulk, [and] later in
the film he loses to Thanos as Banner.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.960
And so, he is left with this reality that…
neither version of himself could stop Thanos.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.220
So, in the five-year time [jump] that happens
early on in Endgame, he sort of dedicates

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.560
himself to trying to better himself so that
this never happens again.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:41.800
And that's sort of the proper motivation for
him to finally figure out how to reconcile

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.020
the two sides of himself."

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:47.360
It was a brutal but necessary outcome for
the mightiest Avenger, and one that will have

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.380
a lasting impact.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.540
The Russos have gone on record stating that,
as far as they're concerned, the Hulk's injuries

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.950
are permanent — meaning that if Banner were
to make any future appearances in the MCU

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:00.420
— which he almost certainly will — he
may be decidedly sidelined when it comes to

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:01.900
throwing down with superpowered adversaries.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.920
We say "may" because, as you may have heard,
the Russos are finished directing MCU pictures

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.620
for the foreseeable future — and it's not
as if another director couldn't come up with

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.510
some plot device by which to heal the Green
Goliath's injuries.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:17.810
Although, should Banner actually remain sidelined
or even fade from the picture entirely, it's

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.930
pretty certain that his legacy will remain.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.620
For the selfless action he took to undo the
deaths of millions, he'll always be among

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.760
the greatest heroes of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe — and somehow, we find it likely

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.060
that moviegoers haven't heard the words "Hulk
smash!" for the last time.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.220
"I think it's gratuitous, but whatever."

